Subjective Cognitive Decline
Undifferentiated or Subclinical Symptom Clusters
Self-reported persistent memory or cognitive worsening | Strictly normal objective neurocognitive testing scores | Fully preserved instrumental ADLs

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: USC-028
Reason for Visit: Acute severe panic attack and agitation related to a perceived massive memory failure, requiring urgent clinical evaluation to rule out acute delirium or early-onset stroke.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and adult offspring
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an urgent assessment was required to evaluate acute-onset confusion and extreme behavioral distress. Immediate clinical examination was mandated to rule out an acute infectious delirium, toxic metabolic encephalopathy, or occult cerebrovascular event, preventing severe clinical deterioration prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I couldn't remember how to use the washing machine today, my brain is completely failing me, and I know I am getting dementia right now.
History of Present Illness: The patient presents in a state of severe acute agitation over a self-reported worsening of memory and thinking over time. There is profound concern regarding cognitive performance completely out of proportion to objective deficits. The adult offspring reports that the patient maintains normal performance on basic activities of daily living and preservation of complex instrumental activities of daily living (managing finances, driving). The patient utilizes mild compensatory behaviors like increased list-making. There is a background of mild underlying anxiety and depressive symptoms without prominent personality changes.
Functional Status: Fully independent.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe acute anxiety and depression.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 148/88 mmHg | HR: 94 bpm | Temp: 36.6 C | RR: 18 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 64 Kg
General: Patient is highly tearful and hyperventilating mildly, expressing catastrophic fears of losing independence. The offspring notes a recent tetrad of unintentional weight loss, functional mobility decline, anorexia, and apathy, pointing strongly to a primary affective disorder driving the cognitive concerns.
Physical Exam: Neurological examination demonstrates an intact orientation to time, place, and person. There are completely normal verbal fluency and language skills. Cranial nerves, motor strength, and sensory functions are intact. A brief bedside cognitive screen (Mini-Cog) is perfect (3/3 recall, normal clock draw). Cardiovascular and respiratory systems are unremarkable. Urinalysis is completely negative.
Environmental Observations: Immaculate home environment. Multiple perfectly organized notepads and calendars present.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient takes no psychotropic medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Subjective Cognitive Decline - Status: Exacerbated by an acute severe anxiety attack and underlying depression.
Differentials:

Acute Delirium (UTI/Infection) - Ruled out definitively due to a perfectly clear sensorium, flawless bedside cognitive testing, clear urinalysis, and normothermia.

Major Neurocognitive Disorder (Dementia) - Ruled out clinically due to the perfect preservation of complex instrumental ADLs, insight, and flawless brief cognitive testing.

Acute Ischemic Stroke - Ruled out due to globally intact neurological examination and fluent, expressive speech.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low.

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient’s sudden, severe behavioral decompensation due to perceived memory loss mandated an urgent after-hours evaluation to exclude an acute delirium or silent neurological insult. Telephone triage correctly prioritized the necessity to evaluate acute confusion in an older adult. A comprehensive physical, neurological, and brief cognitive assessment definitively ruled out acute toxic, metabolic, or ischemic encephalopathy. The objective finding of perfect cognitive screening in the face of catastrophic subjective failure confirmed a primary anxiety/depressive etiology. This definitive clinical intervention rapidly de-escalated the acute panic, secured the patient safely at home, and completely avoided a highly traumatizing and unnecessary emergency department admission.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Conducted extensive psychoeducation and reassurance regarding the negative bedside cognitive screen. The expected investigation results include formal neuropsychological testing demonstrating performance within normal limits for age and education level, which will assist in confirming the diagnosis via outpatient testing.
Positive Behavioral Change: To break the cycle of cognitive anxiety, limit your list-checking behavior to only twice a day (morning and evening); consciously reducing this compulsive safety behavior will train your brain to trust its natural recall abilities and lower your overall anxiety.
Home-Based Services: None indicated tonight.
DME/Safety: None required.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 48 hours for formal depression/anxiety screening and a referral to a clinical psychologist.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient expresses active suicidal ideation with a plan, becomes violently agitated, or develops sudden physical weakness or slurred speech.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive; cognitive functioning must be longitudinally tracked and formally tested in a quiet, non-acute setting.
Clinical Course: The expected natural course involves severe, fluctuating distress regarding memory, heavily linked to underlying mood states and stress levels, rather than rapid neurological decay.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Your family member must seek immediate emergency medical attention if you suddenly become unable to recognize them, if you start seeing things that are not there, or if you become completely unrousable.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: We are not prescribing dementia medications because your objective memory test was absolutely perfect tonight; your brain is simply misfiring due to severe stress and anxiety, creating a false sensation of memory failure.
Actionable Steps: Engage in a highly distracting, enjoyable activity (like watching a favorite movie or calling a friend) immediately after we leave to force your nervous system out of this panic state.

